/target/
# Cargo.lock is intentionally tracked, not ignored: this is a binary (an application), and the
# Rust convention for binaries is to commit the lockfile for reproducible builds - CI builds with
# `--locked`, which needs it present and up to date. (The opposite convention applies to library
# crates, which is probably why this line existed originally.)
